{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D101-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"6c628095b057aad5c23390e484b41233482d0787f52cbbd031c28ad95cc24303\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 delegates the cancellation; A5 makes B say 'funding' and absorb the disappointed silence.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 turns B2's organiser objection into paperwork duty; A5 supplies the explanation B4 anticipates.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 holds the organiser responsible, and B4 raises the choir's practical need for an explanation.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 extends A1's delegated announcement to its explanation and emotional aftermath.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a cancelled choir tour and refund forms, although B's exact role remains open.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'full attention' and A5's delayed entrance both earn their humour through sincere avoidance.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You tell the choir the tour's cancelled. I'll hand out the refund forms.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You're the organiser.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. The forms will need my full attention.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They'll ask why.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Say 'funding' first. I'll join after the disappointed silence.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A recruits B to deliver the emotionally difficult opening while reserving a less exposed supporting job.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Avoiding all responsibility is the closest confusion; this drive specifically delegates the difficult beginning.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 accepts refund work, and A5 promises to join after B has delivered the painful explanation.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses public organisational responsibility and paperwork; b uses a family relationship and a negotiated telephone handover.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged after rereading: B2 and B4 each cause a distinct extension of A's delegation. Internal coach-1.0 rereview remains 92.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B's relationship to the choir is implicit, and B4 adds a foreseeable complication rather than a major reversal.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D101-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a49004b6db674a56972fcca3d41d6757a4af868fda98abc08c8e3b86859296fa\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 delegates the word 'selling'; A5 claims the easier words after that emotional opening.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's request for A with a handover; A5 acknowledges B4 but barely changes the offer.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 invokes Mum's preference, but B4 only identifies the difficulty A has already identified.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 restates A3's division of the conversation without increasing commitment or consequence.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies Mum, a phone call and selling the house as an immediately playable family situation.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's single-word handover is concise; A5's 'words that come after it' repeats that comic premise.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could you phone Mum and begin the conversation about selling the house?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She asked to speak to you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll take over once you've said the word selling.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll put her on speaker. You'll be there for the whole thing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That's fine. Introduce the idea, and when she says my full name, I'll answer.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A gets B to begin a painful family conversation while agreeing to participate once its emotional threshold has been crossed.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D104 also asks another person to speak first, but seeks a frightening admission rather than delegation of an actual difficult conversation.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 requests the call to Mum, and A3 specifies where B's opening should end and A's participation begin.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Unlike a's administrative cover, b accepts unavoidable presence and bargains over who triggers Mum's reaction.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced the repetitive closing exchange. B4 now removes physical escape, causing A5 to negotiate the emotional handover. Internal reread scores fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4: 90. The family house-sale setting is preserved.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 assumes Mum uses A's full name when upset; this is a new illustrative family detail, not inherited provenance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D102-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"63324816a4b2b23fb58a92f28fac80009e4ac3273fa0f8cdac31b9e6f706a026\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 treats 'interesting' as unfinished; A5 treats B4's unexpectedness as another withheld sentence.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 challenges B2's repeated 'interesting'; A5 responds to B4 but gives no specific next demand.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 protect an opinion, but the reason B wants to protect A is unspecified.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 announces progress toward an 'unsent sentence' without pursuing the new clue in B4.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes an audition; A3 gives an actor a playable eye-contact challenge.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'unsent sentence' is polished commentary on the exchange rather than a sharp new interrogation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 75\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You called my audition interesting. What came after that in your head?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You're my sister. I'm trying to be encouraging.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then give me the encouraging half and the half you swallowed.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Your singing was better than your dancing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Better by how much? Are we buying dance lessons or shoes that don't move?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats each tactful assessment as partial disclosure and uses its wording to pursue the omitted judgement.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic criticism-seeking can accept a frank verdict; this drive keeps locating an omission inside each careful answer.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 probes 'interesting'; revised A5 treats B4's comparison as concealing the scale of the dancing problem.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a presses a protective sibling through performance comparisons; b probes a colleague's selective account of a consequential meeting.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The new sibling relationship motivates B's care, and B4 supplies a comparison A5 can interrogate concretely. Internal reread scores 5, 5, 4, 4, 5, 4 in rubric order: 92. Sibling casting and singing versus dancing are new illustrative details.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The shoes option is intentionally comic exaggeration; A must sincerely want to understand the size of the problem.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D102-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"d08c69360e9082fb303c262e5d33ddbb26b92bb91046b743f3a57963b3b605cb\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 asks for the version omitted by careful wording; A3 challenges B2's claim of completeness.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 contradicts B2 with a skipped passage; A5 follows B4's motive but does not pursue the meeting's content.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 wants to spare A distress, but neither person's stake in the meeting becomes concrete.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 calls B4 the 'first unpolished sentence' without increasing the cost or precision of disclosure.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1's meeting and A3's mention of A's name leave the actual decision and relationship undefined.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1's careful/messy contrast is economical, but A5 closes with an abstract description of honesty.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 71\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You said the committee discussed my proposal. What did you leave out?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They had some concerns. I didn't want to ruin your lunch.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Which concern needs protecting me from a sandwich?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They wondered whether you should present it yourself.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Wondered how? Did they suggest coaching me or finding someone with my height and better judgement?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats B's considerate summary as incomplete and extracts increasingly precise versions of the judgement being withheld.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Suspicion of lying would dispute whether the account is true; A instead assumes the careful account leaves something consequential out.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 asks which concern B2 omitted, and A5 interrogates the ambiguity in B4's 'wondered' rather than denying it.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b concerns professional representation and a colleague protecting lunch; a concerns a sibling protecting confidence after an audition.\",\n      \"reason\": \"A committee proposal and lunch replace the unspecified meeting context. B4 now reveals a threat to A's presenting role, which A5 splits into two possible withheld meanings. Internal reread scores 5, 5, 4, 4, 5, 4: 92.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 is more verbally elaborate than ordinary workplace speech and needs an earnest search for information beneath its wit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D103-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e13d96ad5537e562e9fd6868f8fff2921fe8e38af495dfab900f68ca4de8b8e7\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 recruits another attempt despite two failures; A3 requests company but does not clearly recruit belief.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's pessimism with two pencils; A5 answers B4's starting question only metaphorically.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 voices doubt and B4 joins in, but no shared practical stake explains the change.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'bit we still want' adds neither an identifiable goal nor a stronger commitment.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 1, \"evidence\": \"A1 never establishes what the routes are for, and A5 leaves their destination equally undefined.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's two pencils gives a playable object, but A5 remains a general inspirational phrase.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 59\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Draw another route for our parade with me. Two streets said no; the town hasn't.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The only route left goes behind the supermarket.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then take the other pencil. Can you picture people cheering by the loading bay?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"My dad would come. He works there.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Put him on the map. Where would a second person stand?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A recruits B to believe a desired outcome remains possible by building a shared hopeful picture from very thin evidence.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Practical persistence seeks another workable plan; this drive specifically seeks another person's participation in believing it can succeed.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 asks B to picture cheering, and A5 turns B4's single possible spectator into the beginning of a crowd.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a builds hope from a collaborator's tiny concrete lead; b recruits a partner through shared celebration before an uncertain application result.\",\n      \"reason\": \"A parade is a new illustrative setting that makes the route and two pencils literal. B4's father supplies the first believer, prompting A5 to recruit an imagined second spectator. Internal reread scores 5, 5, 4, 4, 5, 4: 92.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The parade route remains only a possibility; the scene must not imply that public access or permission has already been secured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D103-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a6747d1deded7f827d5214b456dc2612bfa21fd809093f7bf02a407b6df034a5\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 invites hopeful preparation before an application result; A3 explicitly wants another person to hope with.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 acknowledges B2's uncertainty, and A5 accepts the two glasses B4 offers.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 joins the preparation, but B2 gives B little personal stake beyond noting the absent result.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 describes B4's glasses as a future without extending the shared hopeful action.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies glasses and an application but leaves the application's purpose and relationship unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's empty place setting is economical imagery; A5 adds a second reflective image rather than a fresh move.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 67\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Help me choose celebration glasses for when our café application gets approved.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We're on the waiting list. I haven't even told my mum.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then just believe it with me here. Which glasses belong in our café?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"These two. Mum would insist on a mug.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Choose her mug too. You just pictured our first customer.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A uses shared preparation for a desired future to recruit B into hope before the available evidence warrants confidence.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Planning a celebration could merely assume success; A actively seeks B's belief after B voices reasons to withhold it.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 explicitly asks B to believe despite B2's waiting list, and A5 develops B4's tentative picture of Mum visiting.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b allows a hesitant partner's family detail to enlarge shared hope; a turns one possible parade spectator into the start of an audience.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The application and celebration glasses are preserved, with a café and waiting list added as illustrative specifics. B4 volunteers Mum's preference, causing A5 to extend the future to a customer. Internal reread scores 5, 5, 4, 4, 5, 4: 92.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The kind of café application remains unspecified, though the waiting list and hoped-for opening supply sufficient playable stakes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D104-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"3767baeb78f6e4f2f5c869d0b6747b378eae090f40238ac94a77d71a052997b1\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 leaves the letter's threat unnamed; A3 asks B to name what A already fears silently.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's warning against assumptions, and A5 acknowledges the shop closure B4 names.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers reassurance and B4 cooperates, but their connection to the shop is unstated.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 observes that the fear is audible without using B4's closure possibility to make another vulnerable request.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 starts with a sentence exercise; the shop situation only arrives in B4.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 clearly expresses the obstacle, but A5 explains the benefit of naming rather than taking a new step.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 71\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before we open the landlord's letter, tell me the worst thing it could mean for our shop.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We've only missed one payment.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I know the number. I need you to say the thing I'm counting towards.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We could lose the shop.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And what would that make me, after Dad trusted me with it? Say that bit first too.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A gets B to name a feared possibility first, then seeks the same protection before voicing its more vulnerable personal meaning.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D101 delegates delivery of difficult news; D104 asks another person to articulate a fear A cannot yet name.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 requests a feared outcome rather than an announcement, and A5 asks B to name the shame attached to B4's possible loss.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a moves from a threatened family shop to personal inadequacy; b moves from competition failure to fear of losing a partner's commitment.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The shop is now present in A1, with landlord, missed payment and Dad added as illustrative stakes. B4's named loss causes A5's deeper request for first naming. Internal reread scores 5, 5, 4, 4, 5, 3: 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 risks inviting B to validate a harsh self-judgement; B remains free to name the fear compassionately or challenge its premise.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D104-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"3e9f84b2fa3feae6c3c231f59249ef5a70372cc5091da59defc18a4f95d3dfde\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 solicits the unspoken competition fear; A3 insists B name it before A does.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's protective reluctance, and A5 follows B4's failure possibility with preparation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants to avoid discouraging A, but B's own connection to qualifying stays implicit.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's generic preparation stops the naming game at its first successful disclosure.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a competition and B4 qualifying, but no particular activity or partnership.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'blank' and 'noun' express the concept neatly but sound more composed than vulnerable.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 73\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before our skating qualifier, can you say the possibility we keep avoiding?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I don't want you carrying that onto the ice.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'm carrying it already. I just need you to say it first.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We might not qualify. I'd still want to skate with you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Why did I need that second sentence so much? Could you name that fear too?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A asks B to voice a frightening possibility before A can acknowledge it, then repeats the request for a deeper relational fear.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D108 seeks another person's vulnerability; here A uses B's words to approach A's own vulnerability.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 says A already carries the fear, and A5 asks B to identify A's need for the reassurance volunteered in B4.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b gives B an active, reassuring partnership role; a focuses on the vulnerable identity attached to a family business.\",\n      \"reason\": \"A skating partnership replaces the unspecified competition. B4 both names failure and promises continued partnership; that promise causes A5 to seek naming of the deeper fear. Internal reread scores 5, 5, 4, 4, 5, 3: 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's reflective phrasing needs a direct emotional address to B so it does not become therapeutic commentary.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D105-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"37d782df17bbb51b8e76d70f1ace381abb88c97bd99da14c42e1c58c5b6a930c\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 identifies crossing-out as restraint's mechanism; A5 explicitly requests recognition of the withheld paragraphs.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 corrects B2's passive 'calmed down'; A5 uses B4's discovery of whole removed paragraphs.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 values the calmer letter, and A5 reveals B is the recipient spared the original attack.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns B4's observation of deletions into a personal debt of appreciation for harm avoided.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 provides two letter versions and visible crossings-out as immediately playable evidence.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5's demand to appreciate paragraphs never received follows precisely from the visible editing in A1.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 88\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Compare the angry letter with the one I sent. Notice the crossing-out.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm glad you calmed down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That crossing-out is how I did it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You removed whole paragraphs.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yes. Please appreciate the paragraphs you never had to receive.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A makes the unseen effort of emotional restraint visible and asks its beneficiary to recognize that effort.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Seeking praise for a good result overlooks the card's focus on what the choice cost or required emotionally.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 redirects B2's approval of calmness toward the active crossing-out, and A5 requests appreciation for the restraint itself.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a presents material evidence of restraint to its beneficiary; b seeks recognition of courage from a person who underestimated a brief contribution.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: the apparent compliment in B2 misreads the effort, and B4's paragraph observation enables the personal claim in A5. Internal coach-1.0 rereview remains 88.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B4 primarily observes rather than bargains, and showing the angry draft may partially undo the restraint A wants recognized.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D105-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a3a2aaa8adf61fef9d89ea357748a3ddc957c25d63bb1f759f88c189b6d373ca\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 contrasts two honest sentences with a morning's effort; A3 reveals the prior inability to say them.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's 'Only two?' with a baseline; A5 acknowledges B4's recognition without developing it.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 underestimates the effort and B4 updates, but the meeting relationship remains generic.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 explains the recognition A wanted after B4 has already supplied it.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 never identifies the meeting's issue or the honest sentences, so their emotional difficulty stays asserted.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's yesterday comparison is concise; A5 restates the lesson about length.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 71\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I told the committee I couldn't chair another year. Those two sentences took all morning.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You sounded completely calm.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I rehearsed them in your parked car until I could stop apologising.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I thought you were waiting for the rain to stop.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I let three dry spells go past. Please count those when you say it was easy.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A asks B to recognize the courage hidden beneath a brief, apparently calm act of setting a boundary.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Reporting anxiety alone does not pursue recognition; A directs B to revise the apparent ease of the choice.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 supplies rehearsal as evidence against B2's impression, and A5 asks B to count the opportunities A could not yet take.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b exposes private preparation for saying no; a displays deleted anger and asks its recipient to appreciate restraint.\",\n      \"reason\": \"A committee chair's refusal and rehearsal in B's car are new illustrative details. B4's mistaken weather explanation prompts A5's measurable evidence of hesitation. Internal reread scores 5, 5, 4, 4, 5, 4: 92.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B says 'calm,' not 'easy'; A5 deliberately exposes A's sensitive interpretation, which should remain sincere rather than accusatory.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D106-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"dd9e99d74415621e6febf1717d2fd66176d65315c3a7f98f08d4f00cbeebec1b\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 explicitly opposes auditioning and avoiding judgement; A5 asks for the other side instead of pressing a choice.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 challenges B2's wish for both at the door, and A5 responds to B4's fear.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 reveals fear, but A1 gives no relationship explaining A's authority or personal investment.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 backs away from A3's doorstep decision into naming an already established desire.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A3 establish an audition doorway, although no immediate decision deadline appears.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 is a concise playable challenge; A5 sounds like a general reflective exercise.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 67\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"They're calling your audition number. Do you want the part enough to let them hear you?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I want it. I don't want them laughing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I can walk you in or drive you home. Which want am I helping?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Walk me in, but promise they won't laugh.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I can promise I'll stay. Is that enough to risk the audition, or are we going home?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A makes B choose whether pursuing a desired role matters more than protection from possible humiliation.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General